    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": "Why Use a Nonlinear Approach?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "From the previous problem, we learned that the linear approach works great when the problem is characterized by a one-to-one relationship."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "However, they may not offer the optimal solution when the elements are characterized by a one-to-many relationship."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Idea Emoji",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "So, the primary limitation of linear data structures is that they cannot naturally represent hierarchical or branching relationships, as each element is limited to exactly one predecessor and one successor."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "This is why we used this figure in our introduction to represent the structure of a family."
    },
    {
      "type": "image",
      "alt": "Using a Tree to Show Relationship Between Elements",
      "src": "data:image/png;base64,"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Since our example consists of a parent having two children (one-to-many), we can't use linear data structures to show the relationship between the elements."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "That's why we chose to use a Tree, which is the first nonlinear data structure we will be covering in our course."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "So, let's get started."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "content": "How does it work?"
    },
